<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hwang Jini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1506">1506</a>–1567</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Hwang Chini, Myŏngwŏl</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hwang Jini was one of the best-known kisaeng of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s Joseon period, who lived during the reign of King Jungjong and was also known by her kisaeng name Myŏngwŏl. Born around 1506, the illegitimate daughter of Hwang Chinsa, a politician's son from a noble family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaesong</a>, and of a woman who was either a kisaeng or of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheonmin">cheonmin</a> status, she became a kisaeng, according to legend, after losing a lover at the age of 15. That life let her learn music, dance, art, literature and poetry, subjects not normally taught to young women. Only a handful of her sijo verses and geomungo pieces survive, many about Kaesong and lost love, and she has become an almost mythic figure in modern Korea.</p></div>
